en by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nUsing the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{186}\\cdot \\sqrt{96}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{2}{13.638\\,182\\cdot 9.797\\,959} \\\\  & ≈0.01\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fbae2f81-ee76-564e-9a95-3a28d9bfac64"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fbae2f81-ee76-564e-9a95-3a28d9bfac64" :answer-field/id #uuid "fbae2f81-ee76-564e-9a95-3a28d9bfac64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2b275c6e-b63d-588b-908a-9be2fcc988a8" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2b275c6e-b63d-588b-908a-9be2fcc988a8" "answer-1d5ed8ea-0d2f-598e-bbee-fa0331eb19ff" "answer-d56f4afa-8ea0-5033-9570-ec6824ffd318" "answer-d9245115-c551-5f31-bb38-69e994a7f681" "answer-fb713398-5c7b-526f-ac30-7b41aa74c8a1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2b275c6e-b63d-588b-908a-9be2fcc988a8" :answer/id #uuid "2b275c6e-b63d-588b-908a-9be2fcc988a8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.01" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1d5ed8ea-0d2f-598e-bbee-fa0331eb19ff" :answer/id #uuid "1d5ed8ea-0d2f-598e-bbee-fa0331eb19ff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.03" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d56f4afa-8ea0-5033-9570-ec6824ffd318" :answer/id #uuid "d56f4afa-8ea0-5033-9570-ec6824ffd318" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.07" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d9245115-c551-5f31-bb38-69e994a7f681" :answer/id #uuid "d9245115-c551-5f31-bb38-69e994a7f681" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.15" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb713398-5c7b-526f-ac30-7b41aa74c8a1" :answer/id #uuid "fb713398-5c7b-526f-ac30-7b41aa74c8a1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.21" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f535de3d-b5b2-5621-843d-e4b9ec9ed404" :question/id #uuid "f535de3d-b5b2-5621-843d-e4b9ec9ed404" :question/math-academy-id "q-134455" :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{13,\\,4,\\,10\\},\\,y=\\{0,\\,-3,\\,9\\}\n$$\n\nYou are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=9$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=2.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient. Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(13-9)(0-2)+(4-9)(-3-2)+(10-9)(9-2) \\\\  & =(4)(-2)+(-5)(-5)+(1)(7) \\\\  & =-8+25+7 \\\\  & =24\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(13-9)^{2}+(4-9)^{2}+(10-9)^{2} \\\\  & =16+25+1 \\\\  & =42 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(0-2)^{2}+(-3-2)^{2}+(9-2)^{2} \\\\  & =4+25+49 \\\\  & =78\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{24}{\\sqrt{42}\\cdot \\sqrt{78}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{24}{6.480\\,741\\cdot 8.831\\,761} \\\\  & ≈0.42\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-52784d35-7cc8-5193-a5b2-29099f33cdec"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-52784d35-7cc8-5193-a5b2-29099f33cdec" :answer-field/id #uuid "52784d35-7cc8-5193-a5b2-29099f33cdec" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-85258801-bb79-5805-a720-da6148b5de85" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4ff9aec8-d155-5665-988f-5d5fbe50f4dd" "answer-0c70e193-480f-5fd4-a75e-269757734d2b" "answer-85258801-bb79-5805-a720-da6148b5de85" "answer-70f793c9-dbdd-5292-816a-30e294057003" "answer-15197980-ac5e-5a3b-abce-35c110615bc3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4ff9aec8-d155-5665-988f-5d5fbe50f4dd" :answer/id #uuid "4ff9aec8-d155-5665-988f-5d5fbe50f4dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.44" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0c70e193-480f-5fd4-a75e-269757734d2b" :answer/id #uuid "0c70e193-480f-5fd4-a75e-269757734d2b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈-0.53" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85258801-bb79-5805-a720-da6148b5de85" :answer/id #uuid "85258801-bb79-5805-a720-da6148b5de85" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.42" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-70f793c9-dbdd-5292-816a-30e294057003" :answer/id #uuid "70f793c9-dbdd-5292-816a-30e294057003" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.46" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15197980-ac5e-5a3b-abce-35c110615bc3" :answer/id #uuid "15197980-ac5e-5a3b-abce-35c110615bc3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho ≈0.51" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-999285af-eeb2-534a-9bcd-a9a462f931b2" :question/id #uuid "999285af-eeb2-534a-9bcd-a9a462f931b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-134454" :question/problem "Consider the following data sets:\n\n$$\nx=\\{5,\\,8,\\,2\\},\\,y=\\{-1,\\,2,\\,5\\}\n$$\n\nYou are given that $\\overset{―}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=2.$ Calculate the corresponding linear correlation coefficient." :question/worked-solution "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(5-5)(-1-2)+(8-5)(2-2)+(2-5)(5-2) \\\\  & =(0)(-3)+(3)(0)+(-3)(3) \\\\  & =0+0-9 \\\\  & =-9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(5-5)^{2}+(8-5)^{2}+(2-5)^{2} \\\\  & =0+9+9 \\\\  & =18 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(-1-2)^{2}+(2-2)^{2}+(5-2)^{2} \\\\  & =9+0+9 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-9)}{\\sqrt{18}\\cdot \\sqrt{18}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{-9}{3\\sqrt{2}\\cdot 3\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-0.5\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bce4a32c-46b5-57a1-9ab0-97e8c5d0e836"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bce4a32c-46b5-57a1-9ab0-97e8c5d0e836" :answer-field/id #uuid "bce4a32c-46b5-57a1-9ab0-97e8c5d0e836" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c6783e59-5110-5183-8ab7-9fe21fa826d3" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4dfd1c2e-ea6f-5434-a4d9-c0b132425dc5" "answer-3aa92092-4369-5ff2-8cce-7258d56ad264" "answer-c6783e59-5110-5183-8ab7-9fe21fa826d3" "answer-74b331b0-c98a-5a41-94f1-cfd562c37a49" "answer-a0660bf7-cc1e-50a6-b15e-2bc59fd44ea8"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4dfd1c2e-ea6f-5434-a4d9-c0b132425dc5" :answer/id #uuid "4dfd1c2e-ea6f-5434-a4d9-c0b132425dc5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho =-0.2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3aa92092-4369-5ff2-8cce-7258d56ad264" :answer/id #uuid "3aa92092-4369-5ff2-8cce-7258d56ad264" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho =-0.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c6783e59-5110-5183-8ab7-9fe21fa826d3" :answer/id #uuid "c6783e59-5110-5183-8ab7-9fe21fa826d3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho =-0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-74b331b0-c98a-5a41-94f1-cfd562c37a49" :answer/id #uuid "74b331b0-c98a-5a41-94f1-cfd562c37a49" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho =0.25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a0660bf7-cc1e-50a6-b15e-2bc59fd44ea8" :answer/id #uuid "a0660bf7-cc1e-50a6-b15e-2bc59fd44ea8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\rho =0.5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-aab6da21-f97b-5ce7-b157-a7c2c5069279" :question/id #uuid "aab6da21-f97b-5ce7-b157-a7c2c5069279" :question/math-academy-id "q-134404" :question/problem "An ergonomist investigates $4$ factories. He wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between $x,$ the money spent by factory owners on safety training courses, and $y,$ the number of accidents per year in the factories. The data is shown below.\n\n| **Money spent (hundreds of $$$)** | **$10$** | **$15$** | **$12$** | **$23$** |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Number of accidents** | $3$ | $6$ | $5$ | $2$ |\n\nYou are given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=15$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=4.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈-0.45$\n\nII. There is a strong negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nHint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation." :question/worked-solution "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. To calculate $\\rho ,$ we first compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(10-15)(3-4)+(15-15)(6-4) \\\\  & +(12-15)(5-4)+(23-15)(2-4) \\\\  & =(-5)(-1)+(0)(2)+(-3)(1)+(8)(-2) \\\\  & =5+0-3-16 \\\\  & =-14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(10-15)^{2}+(15-15)^{2}+(12-15)^{2}+(23-15)^{2} \\\\  & =25+0+9+64 \\\\  & =98 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(3-4)^{2}+(6-4)^{2}+(5-4)^{2}+(2-4)^{2} \\\\  & =1+4+1+4 \\\\  & =10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{-14}{\\sqrt{98}\\cdot \\sqrt{10}} \\\\  & ≈-\\frac{14}{9.899\\,495\\cdot 3.162\\,278} \\\\  & ≈-0.45,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to $2$ decimal places.\n\n- Statement II is false while statement III is true. Since $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is weak. Also, since $\\rho $ is negative, the correlation is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b0ac2d8e-1510-54ca-9445-4aec50f70251"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b0ac2d8e-1510-54ca-9445-4aec50f70251" :answer-field/id #uuid "b0ac2d8e-1510-54ca-9445-4aec50f70251" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-ce4b8373-460f-5dbf-8df7-974dfa84cfd5" :answer-field/choices ["answer-ce4b8373-460f-5dbf-8df7-974dfa84cfd5" "answer-3e641f3d-eaf3-5390-a911-b775f5999ebc" "answer-1f0401d4-997c-5e0e-a92c-bddf630c789e" "answer-fb05b1cb-d912-53a1-83e4-41068da91e3c" "answer-0b9b6447-5e81-5ee1-b93e-f349481ecf21"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ce4b8373-460f-5dbf-8df7-974dfa84cfd5" :answer/id #uuid "ce4b8373-460f-5dbf-8df7-974dfa84cfd5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3e641f3d-eaf3-5390-a911-b775f5999ebc" :answer/id #uuid "3e641f3d-eaf3-5390-a911-b775f5999ebc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1f0401d4-997c-5e0e-a92c-bddf630c789e" :answer/id #uuid "1f0401d4-997c-5e0e-a92c-bddf630c789e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb05b1cb-d912-53a1-83e4-41068da91e3c" :answer/id #uuid "fb05b1cb-d912-53a1-83e4-41068da91e3c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0b9b6447-5e81-5ee1-b93e-f349481ecf21" :answer/id #uuid "0b9b6447-5e81-5ee1-b93e-f349481ecf21" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-5218551b-c171-5334-a3fb-989e7c3fce63" :question/id #uuid "5218551b-c171-5334-a3fb-989e7c3fce63" :question/math-academy-id "q-134359" :question/problem "Olivia plays soccer. She wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between the following variables:\n\n- $x,$ the ages of Olivia's teammates, in years, and\n\n- $y,$ their weights, in kilograms.\n\nYou are given that $\\text{Cov}(x,y)=8,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}=2,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=5.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho =0.8$\n\nII. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" :question/worked-solution "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Calculating the correlation coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{8}{2\\cdot 5} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{5} \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho $ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \" I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6b543cab-3aef-506a-9921-1c34476f23c7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6b543cab-3aef-506a-9921-1c34476f23c7" :answer-field/id #uuid "6b543cab-3aef-506a-9921-1c34476f23c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-3c12122c-52da-5842-87cf-be90d71575fd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3c12122c-52da-5842-87cf-be90d71575fd" "answer-66ce4358-2394-58bb-83f1-93a7b3885b55" "answer-fd88a7c7-483b-5be3-8a8f-267e89a3b634" "answer-e6f10b2f-4613-510b-bee5-7d4497041a13" "answer-76554064-dbbd-545c-8ea3-3674d21496d4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3c12122c-52da-5842-87cf-be90d71575fd" :answer/id #uuid "3c12122c-52da-5842-87cf-be90d71575fd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-66ce4358-2394-58bb-83f1-93a7b3885b55" :answer/id #uuid "66ce4358-2394-58bb-83f1-93a7b3885b55" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fd88a7c7-483b-5be3-8a8f-267e89a3b634" :answer/id #uuid "fd88a7c7-483b-5be3-8a8f-267e89a3b634" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e6f10b2f-4613-510b-bee5-7d4497041a13" :answer/id #uuid "e6f10b2f-4613-510b-bee5-7d4497041a13" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-76554064-dbbd-545c-8ea3-3674d21496d4" :answer/id #uuid "76554064-dbbd-545c-8ea3-3674d21496d4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-382df20d-c998-524f-8214-a4f7f23f788c" :question/id #uuid "382df20d-c998-524f-8214-a4f7f23f788c" :question/math-academy-id "q-134412" :question/problem "A teacher of a group of $4$ students wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between $x,$ the number of hours the students had slept the night before an exam, and $y,$ the student's score in the exam. The data is shown below. \n\n| **Number of hours** | $2$ | $4$ | $6$ | $8$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| **Score ** | $56$ | $68$ | $81$ | $79$ |\n\nYou are given that the means are $\\overset{―}{x}=5$ and $\\overset{―}{y}=71.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈0.81$\n\nII. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" :question/worked-solution "The linear correlation coefficient $\\rho $ of the data sets $x=\\{{x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}\\}$ and $y=\\{{y}_{1},{y}_{2},…,{y}_{n}\\}$ is given by \n\n$$\n\\rho (x,y)=\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}=\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. To calculate $\\rho ,$ we first compute the sum in the numerator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y}) \\\\  & =(2-5)(56-71)+(4-5)(68-71) \\\\  & +(6-5)(81-71)+(8-5)(79-71) \\\\  & =(-3)(-15)+(-1)(-3)+(1)(10)+(3)(8) \\\\  & =45+3+10+24 \\\\  & =82\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSecond, we compute the sums in the denominator:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{xx} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2} \\\\  & =(2-5)^{2}+(4-5)^{2}+(6-5)^{2}+(8-5)^{2} \\\\  & =9+1+1+9 \\\\  & =20 \\\\ {S}_{yy} & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2} \\\\  & =(56-71)^{2}+(68-71)^{2}+(81-71)^{2}+(79-71)^{2} \\\\  & =225+9+100+64 \\\\  & =398\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we use the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{82}{\\sqrt{20}\\cdot \\sqrt{398}} \\\\  & ≈\\frac{82}{4.472\\,136\\cdot 19.949\\,937} \\\\  & ≈0.92,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho $ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-bf65d2d2-724d-5cf1-bd32-eea27646ffc4"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-bf65d2d2-724d-5cf1-bd32-eea27646ffc4" :answer-field/id #uuid "bf65d2d2-724d-5cf1-bd32-eea27646ffc4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-4559fe1f-5cc5-50c5-a69c-e95fdd78cc94" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f514a699-7e5c-5a31-9eaa-07f00c4b3a09" "answer-7cad5610-2a07-5f0c-ae88-c79dd7b2917f" "answer-4559fe1f-5cc5-50c5-a69c-e95fdd78cc94" "answer-b511dbe1-91cf-5909-8045-8b78de98ae64" "answer-c438f6aa-5a1b-51db-ad5a-70f469878ed2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f514a699-7e5c-5a31-9eaa-07f00c4b3a09" :answer/id #uuid "f514a699-7e5c-5a31-9eaa-07f00c4b3a09" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7cad5610-2a07-5f0c-ae88-c79dd7b2917f" :answer/id #uuid "7cad5610-2a07-5f0c-ae88-c79dd7b2917f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4559fe1f-5cc5-50c5-a69c-e95fdd78cc94" :answer/id #uuid "4559fe1f-5cc5-50c5-a69c-e95fdd78cc94" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b511dbe1-91cf-5909-8045-8b78de98ae64" :answer/id #uuid "b511dbe1-91cf-5909-8045-8b78de98ae64" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c438f6aa-5a1b-51db-ad5a-70f469878ed2" :answer/id #uuid "c438f6aa-5a1b-51db-ad5a-70f469878ed2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2fb87a4a-c905-590a-aa16-a1b45daeaaf5" :question/id #uuid "2fb87a4a-c905-590a-aa16-a1b45daeaaf5" :question/math-academy-id "q-134358" :question/problem "The owner of a fast food stall wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between the following variables:\n\n- $x,$ the price per bag of onion rings, in dollars, and\n\n- $y,$ the number of bags of onion rings sold that day.\n\nYou are given that $\\text{Cov}(x,y)=-5,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}=1,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=6.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈-0.83$\n\nII. There is a strong negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" :question/worked-solution "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Calculating the correlation coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{-5}{1\\cdot 6} \\\\  & =\\frac{-5}{6} \\\\  & ≈-0.83.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho $ is negative, the correlation is negative.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-57bf012b-e985-55c0-8315-ed64f981c5f5"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-57bf012b-e985-55c0-8315-ed64f981c5f5" :answer-field/id #uuid "57bf012b-e985-55c0-8315-ed64f981c5f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-865ec0da-3664-5085-9332-c3cb465e50cd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-865ec0da-3664-5085-9332-c3cb465e50cd" "answer-bc99633b-e718-5646-9aa4-66a9e6cccde7" "answer-c1366e14-06ea-55b7-847a-fd6744ad17f0" "answer-eb6e17a1-8c08-51d0-8118-d358e5e1ea2a" "answer-2510127e-62c0-531b-a090-2890bcaa6a0a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-865ec0da-3664-5085-9332-c3cb465e50cd" :answer/id #uuid "865ec0da-3664-5085-9332-c3cb465e50cd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc99633b-e718-5646-9aa4-66a9e6cccde7" :answer/id #uuid "bc99633b-e718-5646-9aa4-66a9e6cccde7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c1366e14-06ea-55b7-847a-fd6744ad17f0" :answer/id #uuid "c1366e14-06ea-55b7-847a-fd6744ad17f0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-eb6e17a1-8c08-51d0-8118-d358e5e1ea2a" :answer/id #uuid "eb6e17a1-8c08-51d0-8118-d358e5e1ea2a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2510127e-62c0-531b-a090-2890bcaa6a0a" :answer/id #uuid "2510127e-62c0-531b-a090-2890bcaa6a0a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b1bd60db-c84d-5039-99c1-be6e7b055360" :question/id #uuid "b1bd60db-c84d-5039-99c1-be6e7b055360" :question/math-academy-id "q-134360" :question/problem "Mark collected information about the weather in his city every day during a particular year. He wants to check the strength of the linear correlation between the following variables:\n\n- $x,$ the temperature, in Celsius degrees, and\n\n- $y,$ the relative humidity, expressed as a percentage.\n\nYou are given that $\\text{Cov}(x,y)=75,$ ${\\sigma }_{x}=14,$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=15.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $\\rho ≈0.44$\n\nII. There is a strong positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\nIII. There is a weak positive correlation between $x$ and $y.$\n\n*Hint: $|\\rho |>0.7$ indicates a strong correlation, and $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7$ indicates a weak correlation.*" :question/worked-solution "The (population) linear correlation coefficient for data sets $x$ and $y$ is given by the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- if $|\\rho |>0.7,$ the correlation is *strong*\n\n- if $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is *weak*\n\n- if $|\\rho |<0.3,$ then the correlation is *negligible*\n\nLet's now examine our statements in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Calculating the correlation coefficient, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{75}{14\\cdot 15} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{14} \\\\  & ≈0.36.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. Since $0.3\\le |\\rho |\\le 0.7,$ the correlation is weak. Also, since $\\rho $ is positive, the correlation is positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-35bce278-6eb6-589e-8ac0-3a4ab76c202d"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-35bce278-6eb6-589e-8ac0-3a4ab76c202d" :answer-field/id #uuid "35bce278-6eb6-589e-8ac0-3a4ab76c202d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d4323d0d-2cc5-57e1-be5e-c2b662bb51ef" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3f88fad8-2eb7-5eee-8188-957d08a6face" "answer-0e4922d4-abcb-5bce-bb9e-6a959e1e184f" "answer-d4b5e441-37d1-5471-8ee9-a00f8c84b34d" "answer-d4323d0d-2cc5-57e1-be5e-c2b662bb51ef" "answer-ea4a039a-0a14-5e0c-b1d8-3bb4461468a6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3f88fad8-2eb7-5eee-8188-957d08a6face" :answer/id #uuid "3f88fad8-2eb7-5eee-8188-957d08a6face" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0e4922d4-abcb-5bce-bb9e-6a959e1e184f" :answer/id #uuid "0e4922d4-abcb-5bce-bb9e-6a959e1e184f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4b5e441-37d1-5471-8ee9-a00f8c84b34d" :answer/id #uuid "d4b5e441-37d1-5471-8ee9-a00f8c84b34d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d4323d0d-2cc5-57e1-be5e-c2b662bb51ef" :answer/id #uuid "d4323d0d-2cc5-57e1-be5e-c2b662bb51ef" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ea4a039a-0a14-5e0c-b1d8-3bb4461468a6" :answer/id #uuid "ea4a039a-0a14-5e0c-b1d8-3bb4461468a6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097862 :tutorial/content "The **linear correlation coefficient**, also called the **Pearson correlation coefficient**, measures the strength of the linear relationship between two variables. It is often denoted by $\\rho$ for a population and by $r$ for a sample. In this lesson, we'll concern ourselves with population data only. \n\nThe linear correlation coefficient satisfies the following constraint:\n\n$$\n-1 \\leq \\rho \\leq 1\n$$\n\nWe interpret the endpoints of this interval as follows:\n\n- $\\rho = 1$ means that there is a perfect *positive* correlation between the data points.\n\n- $\\rho = -1$ means that there is a perfect *negative* correlation between the data points.\n\nMoreover, $\\rho = 0$ means there is no *linear* correlation between the data points. \n\nIn addition, we have the following rule of thumb:\n\n- If $|\\rho| \\gt 0.7,$ the correlation is **strong,** and the data points closely follow a straight line. \n\n![](images/3f/3fd8b4e85bd76bbf78fdb2b135c49b3fd5e37fd9651dcae29d945d75aa45b905.png)\n\n- If $0.3\\leq |\\rho| \\leq 0.7,$ the correlation is **weak.** The data points follow a straight line, but many are far from the line.\n\n![](images/c1/c132ec90770bb84ad6c7dcc4b24773a923139c616f523ba0a23c1506c40c508c.png)\n\n- If $|\\rho| \\lt 0.3,$ then the correlation is **negligible.** There is no (linear) correlation.\n\n![](images/9a/9afa80bfadc40b8b832b17dcea10d66d5a08955909d5bea66c7c010558b5ea19.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097902 :tutorial/content "To calculate $\\rho,$ we use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- $\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)$ is the covariance between $x$ and $y,$ and\n\n- $\\sigma_x$ and $\\sigma_y$ are the standard deviations of $x$ and $y,$ respectively.\n\nFor example, consider data sets $x$ and $y$ such that \n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y)=-9, \\qquad \\sigma_x=6, \\qquad \\sigma_y=2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting our numbers into the formula above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho (x,y) & =\\frac{(-9)}{(6)\\cdot (2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(-9)}{12} \\\\  & =-0.75.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|\\rho| \\gt 0.7,$ the correlation is strong. Also, since $\\rho$ is negative, the correlation is negative.\n\nTherefore, there is a strong negative correlation between $x$ and $y.$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097891 :tutorial/content "Suppose we have data sets $x=\\big\\{ x_1,x_2, \\ldots, x_n \\big\\}$ and $y=\\big\\{ y_1,y_2, \\ldots, y_n \\big\\}$ of the same size.\n\nLet's now introduce the following notation:\n\n$$\nS_{xx} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})^2, \n\\qquad S_{yy} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (y_i-\\overline{y})^2,\n\\qquad S_{xy} = \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y})\n$$\n\nNotice that $S_{xx}, S_{yy},$ and $S_{xy}$ are the sums of square deviations found in the formulas for the variance of $x,$ the variance of $y,$ and covariance of $x$ and $y,$ respectively. \n\nUsing the previous definition, we can show that the correlation coefficient can be calculated as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\\,.\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186097903 :tutorial/content "Let's derive the following result:\n\n$$\n\\rho = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{\\sqrt{S_{xx} \\cdot S_{yy}}}\n$$\n\nWe start with the definition of the correlation coefficient $\\rho\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Cov}(x,y) = \\dfrac1n \\sum\\limits_{i=1}^n (x_i-\\overline{x})(y_i-\\overline{y}) = \\dfrac{S_{xy}}{n}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }_{x} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({x}_{i}-\\overset{―}{x})^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{S}_{xx}}{n}} \\\\ {\\sigma }_{y} & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}({y}_{i}-\\overset{―}{y})^{2}}=\\sqrt{\\frac{{S}_{yy}}{n}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our definition for $\\rho,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\frac{\\text{Cov}(x,y)}{{\\sigma }_{x}\\cdot {\\sigma }_{y}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n})}{\\sqrt{\\frac{{S}_{xx}}{n}}\\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{{S}_{yy}}{n}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n})}{\\sqrt{\\frac{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}{{n}^{2}}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{n})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}{n})} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\cancel{n}})}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}{\\cancel{n}})} \\\\  & =\\frac{{S}_{xy}}{\\sqrt{{S}_{xx}\\cdot {S}_{yy}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186096858 :activity/expected-seconds 780.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092240 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d27eb9e3-1a36-5293-b88b-ba67a6d55870"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186092722 :question/problem "The equation of the normal line to the curve $y=2{x}^{2}$ at the point $(2,8)$ is given by $x+py=q$ where $p$ and $q$ are constants. Find the value of $p+q.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=2{x}^{2}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2})=4x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(2,8):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={f}^{′}(2)=4(2)=8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-8 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2) \\\\ 8y-64 & =-x+2 \\\\ x+8y & =66.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $p+q=8+66=74.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186090532 :answer-field/correct 17592186090534 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-d27eb9e3-1a36-5293-b88b-ba67a6d55870" :question/id #uuid "d27eb9e3-1a36-5293-b88b-ba67a6d55870" :question/math-academy-id "q-342187" :question/problem "Find the equation of the normal line to the curve $y=4x-\\frac{2}{x}$ at the point $(-1,-2).$\n\n$x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$\\cdot \\,y\\,=$\n{{field-2}}" :question/worked-solution "Let $f(x)=4x-\\frac{2}{x}.$ First, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-\\frac{2}{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4x-2{x}^{-1}) \\\\  & =4+2{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =4+\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the slope of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2):$\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(-1)=4+\\frac{2}{(-1)^{2}}=4+2=6\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =-\\frac{1}{6}[x-(-1)] \\\\ -6(y+2) & =x+1 \\\\ -6y-12 & =x+1 \\\\ x+6\\cdot y & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-aa428138-3fdd-52e3-90d7-e57b79a7836a" "answer-field-e1fb6fa9-d25d-5930-b90b-ff3334996e24"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-aa428138-3fdd-52e3-90d7-e57b79a7836a" :answer-field/id #uuid "aa428138-3fdd-52e3-90d7-e57b79a7836a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-e827c707-4183-5bda-8490-ae6373ae9ace" :answer-field/choices ["answer-e827c707-4183-5bda-8490-ae6373ae9ace"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e827c707-4183-5bda-8490-ae6373ae9ace" :answer/id #uuid "e827c707-4183-5bda-8490-ae6373ae9ace" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-e1fb6fa9-d25d-5930-b90b-ff3334996e24" :answer-field/id #uuid "e1fb6fa9-d25d-5930-b90b-ff3334996e24" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-85168c9f-eca2-5f20-86c3-38ab8f99e07f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-85168c9f-eca2-5f20-86c3-38ab8f99e07f"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-85168c9f-eca2-5f20-86c3-38ab8f99e07f" :answer/id #uuid "85168c9f-eca2-5f20-86c3-38ab8f99e07f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-13" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-fad01a8c-be82-5d4b-b516-bfef7fe94dc5" :activity/id #uuid "fad01a8c-be82-5d4b-b516-bfef7fe94dc5" :activity/title "Calculating the Equation of a Normal Line Using Differentiation" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186049119 :activity/course 17592186046647 :activity/steps ["step-273eb5b8-c26e-526c-b817-5fd744a9c5cc" "step-6d424a59-d0d5-5dc4-afe5-f773d34fca45" "step-dfa4a8d0-084b-5540-8862-0031ccbeff76"] :activity/first-step "step-273eb5b8-c26e-526c-b817-5fd744a9c5cc" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-273eb5b8-c26e-526c-b817-5fd744a9c5cc" :step/id #uuid "273eb5b8-c26e-526c-b817-5fd744a9c5cc" :step/content 17592186092510 :step/next "step-6d424a59-d0d5-5dc4-afe5-f773d34fca45" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-6d424a59-d0d5-5dc4-afe5-f773d34fca45" :step/id #uuid "6d424a59-d0d5-5dc4-afe5-f773d34fca45" :step/content 17592186092722 :step/next "step-dfa4a8d0-084b-5540-8862-0031ccbeff76" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-dfa4a8d0-084b-5540-8862-0031ccbeff76" :step/id #uuid "dfa4a8d0-084b-5540-8862-0031ccbeff76" :step/content "question-d27eb9e3-1a36-5293-b88b-ba67a6d55870" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231408 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-f794afec-7639-52ae-b281-3634d3ed8166"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-f794afec-7639-52ae-b281-3634d3ed8166" :question/id #uuid "f794afec-7639-52ae-b281-3634d3ed8166" :question/math-academy-id "q-345241" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $15\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $14\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$ Find, in amperes, the amount of current in the circuit after $0.3$ seconds, given that the initial charge on the capacitor is $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ and the initial current is $2\\,\\text{A}.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/e4/e44f91621134e91a9f742811897940b87ffc24bea4b15100e43d2aa15a8c4330.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=14\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=14I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{\\frac{1}{24}}=24q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n15=14I+24q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(15) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(14I+24q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ 0 & =14\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+24\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+7\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}+12I=0.\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear homogeneous equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must take a linear combination of two linearly independent solutions.\n\nAssuming the solutions take the form $I={e}^{\\lambda t},$ we obtain two solutions, namely, $I={e}^{-3t}$ and $I={e}^{-4t}.$\nSo, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-3t}+B{e}^{-4t}.\n$$\n\nWe're told that the circuit starts with an initial charge of $\\frac{1}{4}\\,\\text{C}$ on the capacitor and an initial current of $2\\,\\text{A}.$\nNow, solving the Kirchhoff's law equation for $\\frac{dI}{dt},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{dI}{dt}=\\frac{15}{2}-7I-12q.\n$$\n\nSo, we can apply the initial conditions $I(0)=2$ and \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(0) & =\\frac{15}{2}-7I(0)-12q(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}-7\\cdot 2-12\\cdot \\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{19}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand solve for $A$ and $B.$\n\n- Substituting $I(0)=2$ into our general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =A{e}^{-0}+B{e}^{-0} \\\\ 2 & =A+B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Differentiating $I,$ we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}=-3A{e}^{-3t}-4B{e}^{-4t}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in ${I}^{′}(0)=-\\frac{19}{2}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{19}{2} & =-3A{e}^{-0}-4B{e}^{-0} \\\\ -\\frac{19}{2} & =-3A-4B.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPutting the two equations in $A$ and $B$ together gives the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}2=A+B \\\\ -\\frac{19}{2}=-3A-4B\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $A=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $B=\\frac{7}{2}.$ Therefore, the equation for the current (in amperes) in the circuit is\n\n$$\nI(t)=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-3t}+\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{-4t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the current in the circuit after $0.3$ seconds, in amperes, is\n\n$$\nI(0.3)=-\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{-0.9}+\\frac{7}{2}{e}^{-1.2}≈0.444,\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8c9be830-6e08-534d-b109-3e3cae593612"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8c9be830-6e08-534d-b109-3e3cae593612" :answer-field/id #uuid "8c9be830-6e08-534d-b109-3e3cae593612" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e4ca53e1-b5a2-5e61-8851-027807dd3996" :answer-field/choices ["answer-72a63e5b-d23c-553a-9911-becbdc9a9fef" "answer-f3989d66-7541-59d0-ab98-8dbe3300e10f" "answer-e4ca53e1-b5a2-5e61-8851-027807dd3996" "answer-bafd3dc4-09cb-5e09-8f79-24e4ff33b5f6" "answer-c75d9082-3f48-549d-a710-9255637e1bec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72a63e5b-d23c-553a-9911-becbdc9a9fef" :answer/id #uuid "72a63e5b-d23c-553a-9911-becbdc9a9fef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.382" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f3989d66-7541-59d0-ab98-8dbe3300e10f" :answer/id #uuid "f3989d66-7541-59d0-ab98-8dbe3300e10f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.407" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e4ca53e1-b5a2-5e61-8851-027807dd3996" :answer/id #uuid "e4ca53e1-b5a2-5e61-8851-027807dd3996" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.444" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bafd3dc4-09cb-5e09-8f79-24e4ff33b5f6" :answer/id #uuid "bafd3dc4-09cb-5e09-8f79-24e4ff33b5f6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.515" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c75d9082-3f48-549d-a710-9255637e1bec" :answer/id #uuid "c75d9082-3f48-549d-a710-9255637e1bec" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0.601" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186231409 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-8da21d86-c5a3-5a9d-afe5-a4fc0cdf2ebd"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-8da21d86-c5a3-5a9d-afe5-a4fc0cdf2ebd" :question/id #uuid "8da21d86-c5a3-5a9d-afe5-a4fc0cdf2ebd" :question/math-academy-id "q-345408" :question/problem "![](images/8a/8a4951e6e156fad815b6b21376031b003f61a986a399bed712709272c1ae6409.png)\n\nConsider the RCL circuit shown above. Given that the current $I(t)$ in the circuit at time $t$ seconds is given by\n\n$$\nI(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{P}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{Q}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A,$ $B,$ $P,$ and $Q$ are constants, find the value of $P+Q.$\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*" :question/worked-solution "The diagram shows a series RCL circuit with a source electromotive force (EMF) of $4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,\\text{V},$ a resistor of resistance $6\\,Ω,$ a capacitor of capacitance $0.25\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductor of inductance $2\\,\\text{H}.$\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops.\n\nIn an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=6\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=6I.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{0.25}=4q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=2\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, these values are related by the equation\n\n$$\n4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)=6I+4q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $t,$ the differential equation governing the current in the circuit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dt}(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) & =\\frac{d}{dt}(6I+4q+2\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}) \\\\ -8\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =6\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+2\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich, when substituting in $I=\\frac{dq}{dt},$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}I}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{dI}{dt}+2I=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nThe given equation is a second-order linear equation with constant coefficients. To find the general solution, we must sum the complementary and particular solutions.\n\n- To find the complementary solution ${I}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{c}}{d{t}^{2}}+3\\cdot \\frac{d{I}_{c}}{dt}+2{I}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving using an integration technique, such as a trial solution, we obtain the complementary solution\n\n$$\n{I}_{c}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants.\n\n- Next, we find a particular solution. Note that the right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is sinusoidal with argument $2t.$ So, we assume that the particular solution is also sinusoidal with argument $2t,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute this, as well as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d{I}_{p}}{dt} & =-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t), \\\\ \\frac{{d}^{2}{I}_{p}}{d{t}^{2}} & =-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\ninto the inhomogeneous equation and group like terms on the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-4\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)-4\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))+3(-2\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\beta \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ +2(\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\beta \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) \\\\ (-2\\alpha +6\\beta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+(-6\\alpha -2\\beta )\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t) & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha +6\\beta =0\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)) \\\\ -6\\alpha -2\\beta =-4\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =\\frac{3}{5}$ and $\\beta =\\frac{1}{5}.$ Therefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{I}_{p}(t)=\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by the sum of the complementary solution and the particular solution, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\nI(t)={I}_{c}(t)+{I}_{p}(t)=A{e}^{-t}+B{e}^{-2t}+\\frac{3}{5}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have $P=3$ and $Q=1,$ and\n\n$$\nP+Q=3+1=4.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-139a8209-37f8-5a7e-ba36-31b603338e18"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-139a8209-37f8-5a7e-ba36-31b603338e18" :answer-field/id #uuid "139a8209-37f8-5a7e-ba36-31b603338e18" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-861a18cf-294c-5905-ab08-6ea6cd3015fd" :answer-field/choices ["answer-af7e31de-acb4-51be-b18b-2a28da918ffb" "answer-2a1cb880-662d-55c8-a281-13e3b6d4224a" "answer-861a18cf-294c-5905-ab08-6ea6cd3015fd" "answer-303deaed-64c3-5a32-a361-880f41251d31" "answer-09a1db72-7a2f-5408-a309-33ae06cdd204"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af7e31de-acb4-51be-b18b-2a28da918ffb" :answer/id #uuid "af7e31de-acb4-51be-b18b-2a28da918ffb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2a1cb880-662d-55c8-a281-13e3b6d4224a" :answer/id #uuid "2a1cb880-662d-55c8-a281-13e3b6d4224a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-861a18cf-294c-5905-ab08-6ea6cd3015fd" :answer/id #uuid "861a18cf-294c-5905-ab08-6ea6cd3015fd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-303deaed-64c3-5a32-a361-880f41251d31" :answer/id #uuid "303deaed-64c3-5a32-a361-880f41251d31" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-09a1db72-7a2f-5408-a309-33ae06cdd204" :answer/id #uuid "09a1db72-7a2f-5408-a309-33ae06cdd204" :answer/type :answ