gned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(13x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(13x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-8547732e-e542-5b11-97a7-baefd3b98279"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-8547732e-e542-5b11-97a7-baefd3b98279" :answer-field/id #uuid "8547732e-e542-5b11-97a7-baefd3b98279" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-b224bbfe-b93a-56bf-919b-ef26884dc6ed" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8d915675-e654-5197-9282-adb454d2ab25" "answer-fc0b6a9c-9e17-517e-88a9-ce6797203a18" "answer-95f3a3c1-143d-5338-afc7-5b14662fe7cf" "answer-b224bbfe-b93a-56bf-919b-ef26884dc6ed" "answer-3317ccb1-01b9-5adb-87ce-11acf44e2926"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8d915675-e654-5197-9282-adb454d2ab25" :answer/id #uuid "8d915675-e654-5197-9282-adb454d2ab25" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)-\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(13x)+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc0b6a9c-9e17-517e-88a9-ce6797203a18" :answer/id #uuid "fc0b6a9c-9e17-517e-88a9-ce6797203a18" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(13x)-\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-95f3a3c1-143d-5338-afc7-5b14662fe7cf" :answer/id #uuid "95f3a3c1-143d-5338-afc7-5b14662fe7cf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)+\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(13x)+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b224bbfe-b93a-56bf-919b-ef26884dc6ed" :answer/id #uuid "b224bbfe-b93a-56bf-919b-ef26884dc6ed" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-\\frac{1}{26}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(13x)+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3317ccb1-01b9-5adb-87ce-11acf44e2926" :answer/id #uuid "3317ccb1-01b9-5adb-87ce-11acf44e2926" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)-\\frac{1}{13}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(13x)+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-2194316f-56dc-52e2-8be5-47b08dc370f4" :question/id #uuid "2194316f-56dc-52e2-8be5-47b08dc370f4" :question/math-academy-id "q-340478" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-4x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x+4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(-3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{3\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5x)]}_{0}^{3\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{10}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{15\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(1-0)-\\frac{1}{10}(-1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{15}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9b988de4-ecca-56c2-b6bc-fc57b5242fce"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9b988de4-ecca-56c2-b6bc-fc57b5242fce" :answer-field/id #uuid "9b988de4-ecca-56c2-b6bc-fc57b5242fce" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "answer-52f3b306-9a74-5821-a7de-b9abe78cf733" :answer-field/choices ["answer-52f3b306-9a74-5821-a7de-b9abe78cf733"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52f3b306-9a74-5821-a7de-b9abe78cf733" :answer/id #uuid "52f3b306-9a74-5821-a7de-b9abe78cf733" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{4}{15}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167279 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta )-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x-6x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x+6x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0))-\\frac{1}{18}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(-1-0)-\\frac{1}{18}(1-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164892 :answer-field/correct "answer-fb997472-86c5-5cad-89b9-8ea1ca0030a0" :answer-field/choices ["answer-fb997472-86c5-5cad-89b9-8ea1ca0030a0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fb997472-86c5-5cad-89b9-8ea1ca0030a0" :answer/id #uuid "fb997472-86c5-5cad-89b9-8ea1ca0030a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167281 :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x=$ {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x+6x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x-6x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(9x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{18}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(9x)+\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{9\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0))+\\frac{1}{6}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{18}(0-1)+\\frac{1}{6}(0-1) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186164899 :answer-field/correct "answer-825d9664-8352-5088-b23a-b2777e89df08" :answer-field/choices ["answer-825d9664-8352-5088-b23a-b2777e89df08"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-825d9664-8352-5088-b23a-b2777e89df08" :answer/id #uuid "825d9664-8352-5088-b23a-b2777e89df08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-85e8fc1d-5dff-59c4-83a1-b0b53e9abc4c" :question/id #uuid "85e8fc1d-5dff-59c4-83a1-b0b53e9abc4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-340482" :question/problem "$\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get the following product-to-sum identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta )+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x+4x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x-4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(14x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the integral gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(14x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{28}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-eae38ee7-3db9-5846-94ff-52291ad80ef9"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-eae38ee7-3db9-5846-94ff-52291ad80ef9" :answer-field/id #uuid "eae38ee7-3db9-5846-94ff-52291ad80ef9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-0992dba4-8f7b-5bf3-9cee-a37efc4d1f62" :answer-field/choices ["answer-75c05976-5265-5960-b4e0-6e7a245f9c37" "answer-78292b8f-ac5e-58bb-aead-4c163faa0593" "answer-b94602f1-fd76-5241-944c-f65add77d8e5" "answer-0992dba4-8f7b-5bf3-9cee-a37efc4d1f62" "answer-94dc8ec7-7293-5c84-a872-14c82724d44c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-75c05976-5265-5960-b4e0-6e7a245f9c37" :answer/id #uuid "75c05976-5265-5960-b4e0-6e7a245f9c37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{14}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(14x)-\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-78292b8f-ac5e-58bb-aead-4c163faa0593" :answer/id #uuid "78292b8f-ac5e-58bb-aead-4c163faa0593" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b94602f1-fd76-5241-944c-f65add77d8e5" :answer/id #uuid "b94602f1-fd76-5241-944c-f65add77d8e5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{28}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0992dba4-8f7b-5bf3-9cee-a37efc4d1f62" :answer/id #uuid "0992dba4-8f7b-5bf3-9cee-a37efc4d1f62" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{28}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(14x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-94dc8ec7-7293-5c84-a872-14c82724d44c" :answer/id #uuid "94dc8ec7-7293-5c84-a872-14c82724d44c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{28}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(14x)+\\frac{1}{24}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)+C" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168040 :tutorial/content "When an integrand contains a product of trigonometric functions, we can often rewrite that product as a sum using the sum and difference formulas. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta) + \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta) = 2 \\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta,$ we obtain the following **product-to-sum identity**:\n\n$$\n\\cos \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)+\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\cos(5x)\\cos(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x-3x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x+3x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)+\\frac{1}{16}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168041 :tutorial/content "Similar to the above, when an integrand contains a product of sines, we can rewrite that product as a sum of cosines using the sum and difference formulas for cosine. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubtracting the second equation from the first, we get\n\n$$\n\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta) - \\cos(\\alpha+\\beta) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta,$ we obtain the following *product-to-sum identity*:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\alpha \\sin \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\cos(\\alpha-\\beta)-\\cos(\\alpha+\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(7x)\\sin(4x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x-4x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(7x+4x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(7x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(11x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{1}{22}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(11x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186168042 :tutorial/content "Next, when an integrand contains a product of sine and cosine, we can rewrite that product as a sum of sines using the sum and difference formulas for sine. Then, the integral becomes a sum of simpler integrals.\n\nFirst, let's recall the sum and difference formulas for sine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha +\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta  \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\alpha -\\beta ) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\beta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\alpha \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\beta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these equations, we get\n\n$$\n\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta) + \\sin(\\alpha-\\beta) = 2 \\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta.\n$$\n\nNow, solving for $\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta,$ we obtain the following *product-to-sum identity*:\n\n$$\n\\sin \\alpha \\cos \\beta = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left(\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)+\\sin(\\alpha-\\beta)\\right)\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\int \\sin(8x)\\cos(2x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nUsing the identity above,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x) & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x+2x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x-2x)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(8x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(10x)+\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(6x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{20}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(10x)-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6x)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186167004 :activity/expected-seconds 1140.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
